<div id="outline-container-devd-settings" class="outline-2">
<h2 id="devd-settings">Intercepting functional key presses with devd</h2>
<div class="outline-text-2" id="text-devd-settings">
<p>
First of all, it is necessary to understand which events are
intercepted by <code>devd</code> when user presses the mentioned functional keys.
So, I stopped the <code>devd</code> daemon and launch it from a <code>root</code> user with <code>-d</code>
argument — to look at the events printed in the terminal. E.g. this is
how looks like the event for <code>Fn+F2</code> keypress:
</p>
<pre class="example">
Popping table
Processing event '!system=ACPI subsystem=IBM type=\_SB_.PCI0.LPCB.EC__.HKEY notify=0x02'
Pushing table
setting *=!system=ACPI subsystem=IBM type=\_SB_.PCI0.LPCB.EC__.HKEY notify=0x02
setting _=system=ACPI subsystem=IBM type=\_SB_.PCI0.LPCB.EC__.HKEY notify=0x02
setting timestamp=1770849066.826698
setting vm_guest=none
setting system=ACPI
setting subsystem=IBM
setting type=\_SB_.PCI0.LPCB.EC__.HKEY
setting notify=0x02
Processing notify event
</pre>

<p>
So, after some experimenting with keys, which I want to use, I got the
next codes:
</p>
<table border="2" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="6" frame="void">


<colgroup>
<col  class="org-left" />

<col  class="org-right" />
</colgroup>
<thead>
<tr>
<th scope="col" class="org-left">Buttons</th>
<th scope="col" class="org-right">notify code</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td class="org-left">Fn+F2</td>
<td class="org-right">0x02</td>
</tr>

<tr>
<td class="org-left">Fn+F3</td>
<td class="org-right">0x03</td>
</tr>

<tr>
<td class="org-left">Fn+F5</td>
<td class="org-right">0x05</td>
</tr>

<tr>
<td class="org-left">Fn+Space</td>
<td class="org-right">0x14</td>
</tr>

<tr>
<td class="org-left">ThinkVantage</td>
<td class="org-right">0x18</td>
</tr>

<tr>
<td class="org-left">Mic toggle</td>
<td class="org-right">0x1b</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>
The other event parameters are the same for all the keys:
</p>
<pre class="example">
system=ACPI
subsystem=IBM
type=\_SB_.PCI0.LPCB.EC__.HKEY
</pre>

<p>
Next steps are simple — just create a rule for <code>devd</code>, which intercepts
the desired events and calls a some command — the most simple way is
just to call the shell-script with pressed key name as an argument.
</p>

<p>
I placed the <code>devd</code> rules for buttons to the
<code>/usr/local/etc/devd/thinkpad.conf</code> (see the <code>man 5 devd.conf</code> for syntax
explanation):
</p>
<pre class="example">
notify 100 {
       match "system"      "ACPI";
       match "subsystem"   "IBM";
       match "type"        "\\_SB_.PCI0.LPCB.EC__.HKEY";
       match "notify"      "0x02";
       action "/root/bin/thinkpad_keys.tcsh lock";
};

notify 100 {
       match "system"      "ACPI";
       match "subsystem"   "IBM";
       match "type"        "\\_SB_.PCI0.LPCB.EC__.HKEY";
       match "notify"      "0x03";
       action "/root/bin/thinkpad_keys.tcsh batt";
};

notify 100 {
       match "system"      "ACPI";
       match "subsystem"   "IBM";
       match "type"        "\\_SB_.PCI0.LPCB.EC__.HKEY";
       match "notify"      "0x05";
       action "/root/bin/thinkpad_keys.tcsh radio";
};

notify 100 {
       match "system"      "ACPI";
       match "subsystem"   "IBM";
       match "type"        "\\_SB_.PCI0.LPCB.EC__.HKEY";
       match "notify"      "0x14";
       action "/root/bin/thinkpad_keys.tcsh space";
};

notify 100 {
       match "system"      "ACPI";
       match "subsystem"   "IBM";
       match "type"        "\\_SB_.PCI0.LPCB.EC__.HKEY";
       match "notify"      "0x18";
       action "/root/bin/thinkpad_keys.tcsh thinkvantage";
};

notify 100 {
       match "system"      "ACPI";
       match "subsystem"   "IBM";
       match "type"        "\\_SB_.PCI0.LPCB.EC__.HKEY";
       match "notify"      "0x1b";
       action "/root/bin/thinkpad_keys.tcsh mictoggle";
};
</pre>

<p>
Then, restarted devd with <code>service devd restart</code> command.
</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="outline-container-shell-script-key-presses" class="outline-2">
<h2 id="shell-script-key-presses">Processing functional keypresses with shell script</h2>
<div class="outline-text-2" id="text-shell-script-key-presses">
<p>
As I mentioned before, the script just receives some string as an
argument when user presses some function key. The strings are the
next:
</p>
<ul class="org-ul">
<li><code>"lock"</code> for Fn+F2</li>
<li><code>"batt"</code> for Fn+F3</li>
<li><code>"radio"</code> for Fn+F5</li>
<li><code>"space"</code> for Fn+Space</li>
<li><code>"thinkvantage"</code> for blue ThinkVantage button</li>
<li><code>"mictoggle"</code> for "Mic toggle" button.</li>
</ul>

<p>
Dispatching of execution flow with these string is simple — just check
what we received an argument and it is not empty, then use <code>switch case</code>
for tcsh script:
</p>
<div class="org-src-container">
<pre class="src src-tcsh"><span style="color: #909995;">#</span><span style="color: #909995;">!/bin/</span><span style="color: #489100; font-weight: bold;">tcsh</span>

<span style="color: #489100; font-weight: bold;">if</span> ( { <span style="color: #53676d; font-weight: bold;">test</span> <span style="color: #009c8f;">"$1"</span> } == 0 ) <span style="color: #489100; font-weight: bold;">then</span>
        <span style="color: #489100; font-weight: bold;">exit</span> 2
<span style="color: #489100; font-weight: bold;">else if</span> ( { <span style="color: #53676d; font-weight: bold;">test</span> -z <span style="color: #009c8f;">"$1"</span> } ) <span style="color: #489100; font-weight: bold;">then</span>
        <span style="color: #489100; font-weight: bold;">exit</span> 1
endif

<span style="color: #0072d4;">switch</span> ( $<span style="color: #0072d4;">1</span> )
        <span style="color: #489100; font-weight: bold;">case</span> <span style="color: #009c8f;">"lock"</span>:
                breaksw
        <span style="color: #489100; font-weight: bold;">case</span> <span style="color: #009c8f;">"batt"</span>:
                breaksw
        <span style="color: #489100; font-weight: bold;">case</span> <span style="color: #009c8f;">"radio"</span>:
                breaksw
        <span style="color: #489100; font-weight: bold;">case</span> <span style="color: #009c8f;">"space"</span>:
                breaksw
        <span style="color: #489100; font-weight: bold;">case</span> <span style="color: #009c8f;">"thinkvantage"</span>:
                breaksw
        <span style="color: #489100; font-weight: bold;">case</span> <span style="color: #009c8f;">"mictoggle"</span>:
                breaksw
        default:
                breaksw
endsw
</pre>
</div>

<p>
Then, in each <code>case</code> the necessary program is launched. I didn't come up
with actions for <code>Fn+F3</code> and <code>Fn+F5</code> buttons, but:
</p>
<ul class="org-ul">
<li><code>Fn+F2</code> obviously locks screen with xlock</li>
<li><code>Fn+F5</code> launches <code>wifimgr</code> — the GTK2 frontend for <code>wpa_supplicant</code></li>
<li><code>Fn+Space</code> launches <code>xmag</code> — magnifier tool for X server</li>
<li>"Mic toggle" button — obviously toggles the microphone state via the
<code>mixer</code>.</li>
</ul>

<div class="org-src-container">
<pre class="src src-tcsh"><span style="color: #489100; font-weight: bold;">case</span> <span style="color: #009c8f;">"lock"</span>:
    setenv DISPLAY :0
    xhost +local:root
    mdo -u drag0n /bin/tcsh -c <span style="color: #009c8f;">"setenv DISPLAY :0 &amp;&amp; /usr/local/bin/xlock &amp;"</span>
    breaksw
<span style="color: #489100; font-weight: bold;">case</span> <span style="color: #009c8f;">"batt"</span>:
    breaksw
<span style="color: #489100; font-weight: bold;">case</span> <span style="color: #009c8f;">"radio"</span>:
    setenv DISPLAY :0
    setenv GDK_SCALE 0.5
    setenv GDK_DPI_SCALE 2
    xhost +local:root
    /usr/local/bin/wifimgr &amp;
    breaksw
<span style="color: #489100; font-weight: bold;">case</span> <span style="color: #009c8f;">"space"</span>:
    setenv DISPLAY :0
    xhost +local:root
    mdo -u drag0n /bin/tcsh -c <span style="color: #ad8900; font-weight: bold;">\</span>
        <span style="color: #009c8f;">"setenv DISPLAY :0 &amp;&amp; /usr/local/bin/xmag -source 512x512 -mag 3 &amp;"</span>
    breaksw
<span style="color: #489100; font-weight: bold;">case</span> <span style="color: #009c8f;">"thinkvantage"</span>:
    breaksw
<span style="color: #489100; font-weight: bold;">case</span> <span style="color: #009c8f;">"mictoggle"</span>:
    mixer mic.mute=toggle
    breaksw
</pre>
</div>

<p>
<b>Note:</b> to launch X applications it is necessary to set the proper
<code>DISPLAY</code> variable for <code>root</code> first. And also allow localhost connections
from root to my user's X server via the <code>xhost</code>. Also, there are some
environment variables necessary for GTK applications.
</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="outline-container-acpi-ibm-led" class="outline-2">
<h2 id="acpi-ibm-led">ACPI IBM module settings for LEDs</h2>
<div class="outline-text-2" id="text-acpi-ibm-led">
<p>
OK, for now I'm able to toggle microphone state with the corresponding
button. But, the LED on this button is still not in use.
</p>

<p>
Fortunately, <code>acpi_ibm</code> kernel module comes to the rescue. It provides
one useful sysctl variable: <code>dev.acpi_ibm.0.mic_led</code>. The next command
could be used to light up the LED on the "Mic toggle" button:
</p>
<pre class="example">
doas sysctl dev.acpi_ibm.0.mic_led=1
</pre>

<p>
And the next command turns off the LED:
</p>
<pre class="example">
doas sysctl dev.acpi_ibm.0.mic_led=0
</pre>

<p>
So, to match LED state with real state of microphone the next lines
were added to the aforementioned shell script for <code>devd</code>:
</p>
<div class="org-src-container">
<pre class="src src-tcsh"><span style="color: #489100; font-weight: bold;">case</span> <span style="color: #009c8f;">"mictoggle"</span>:
    mixer mic.mute=toggle
    <span style="color: #489100; font-weight: bold;">if</span> ( { <span style="color: #53676d; font-weight: bold;">test</span> <span style="color: #8762c6; font-weight: bold;">`mixer mic.mute`</span> == <span style="color: #009c8f;">"mic.mute=on"</span> } ) <span style="color: #489100; font-weight: bold;">then</span>
        sysctl dev.acpi_ibm.0.mic_led=1
    <span style="color: #489100; font-weight: bold;">else</span>
        sysctl dev.acpi_ibm.0.mic_led=0
    endif
    breaksw
</pre>
</div>

<p>
One little addition is left. That if I turn off the mic, then
powercycle the laptop? The microphone state will be saved by the OS,
but the LED will be obviously turned off. To synchronize mic and LED
states, I used this damn simple startup service:
</p>
<div class="org-src-container">
<pre class="src src-shell"><span style="color: #909995;">#</span><span style="color: #909995;">!/bin/</span><span style="color: #489100; font-weight: bold;">sh</span>
<span style="color: #909995;">#</span>
<span style="color: #909995;"># </span><span style="color: #909995;">PROVIDE: micled</span>
<span style="color: #909995;"># </span><span style="color: #909995;">REQUIRE: DAEMON</span>
<span style="color: #909995;"># </span><span style="color: #909995;">KEYWORD: nojail shutdown</span>
<span style="color: #909995;">#</span>

. /etc/rc.subr

<span style="color: #0072d4;">name</span>=<span style="color: #009c8f;">"micled"</span>
<span style="color: #0072d4;">desc</span>=<span style="color: #009c8f;">"Synchronize mic and LED states"</span>
<span style="color: #0072d4;">rcvar</span>=<span style="color: #009c8f;">"micled_enable"</span>
load_rc_config $<span style="color: #0072d4;">name</span>

: ${<span style="color: #0072d4;">micled_enable</span>:=<span style="color: #009c8f;">"NO"</span>}

<span style="color: #0072d4;">start_cmd</span>=<span style="color: #009c8f;">"${name}_sync"</span>
<span style="color: #0072d4;">stop_cmd</span>=<span style="color: #009c8f;">"${name}_sync"</span>
<span style="color: #0072d4;">restart_cmd</span>=<span style="color: #009c8f;">"${name}_sync"</span>
<span style="color: #0072d4;">status_cmd</span>=<span style="color: #009c8f;">"${name}_sync"</span>

<span style="color: #0072d4;">micled_sync</span>()
{
    <span style="color: #489100; font-weight: bold;">if</span> [ $(mixer mic.mute) == <span style="color: #009c8f;">"mic.mute=on"</span> ]; <span style="color: #489100; font-weight: bold;">then</span>
        sysctl dev.acpi_ibm.0.mic_led=1 &gt; /dev/null
    <span style="color: #489100; font-weight: bold;">else</span>
        sysctl dev.acpi_ibm.0.mic_led=0 &gt; /dev/null
    <span style="color: #489100; font-weight: bold;">fi</span>
    <span style="color: #53676d; font-weight: bold;">echo</span> <span style="color: #009c8f;">"Mic state synced with LED"</span>
}

run_rc_command <span style="color: #009c8f;">"$1"</span>
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<div id="outline-container-thinklight-morse-sos" class="outline-3">
<h3 id="thinklight-morse-sos">(Bonus section) Alert with Morse code and ThinkLight</h3>
<div class="outline-text-3" id="text-thinklight-morse-sos">
<p>
The <code>acpi_ibm</code> could not only operate with Thinkpad specific things via
sysctl variables. It also provides the <a href="https://man.freebsd.org/cgi/man.cgi?query=led&amp;sektion=4&amp;manpath=FreeBSD+15.0-RELEASE+and+Ports">led(4)</a> interface to the
ThinkLight!
</p>


<div class="figure">
<p><img src="/assets/images/stickers/d3shik_drgn-surprised.png" alt="d3shik_drgn-surprised.png" align="center" width="20%" />
</p>
<p class="fignum"><i>Author: D3shik (<a href="https://t.me/dashihome">https://t.me/dashihome</a>)</i></p>
</div>

<p>
So, with the help of <a href="https://man.freebsd.org/cgi/man.cgi?query=morse&amp;apropos=0&amp;sektion=6&amp;manpath=FreeBSD+15.0-RELEASE+and+Ports&amp;format=html">morse(6)</a> program it is possible to transmit
messages with the speed of light. For example, to signal "SOS" with
ThinkLight this command, launched from <code>root</code>, could be used:
</p>
<pre class="example">
morse -l "SOS" &gt; /dev/led/thinklight
</pre>

<p>
The ThinkLight will blink "SOS" signal indefinitely. To stop it and
turn off ThinkLight the next command is necessary:
</p>
<pre class="example">
echo "0" &gt; /dev/led/thinklight
</pre>

<p>
Thusly, I decided to use this little feature to signal about low
battery level. Because the big red notification on the center of the
screen is not enough for me.
</p>

<p>
This script is used to blink "SOS" three times by ThinkLight then turn
off the LED:
</p>
<div class="org-src-container">
<pre class="src src-shell"><span style="color: #909995;">#</span><span style="color: #909995;">!/bin/</span><span style="color: #489100; font-weight: bold;">sh</span>

/usr/bin/morse -l <span style="color: #009c8f;">"SOS"</span> &gt; /dev/led/thinklight
sleep 13
<span style="color: #53676d; font-weight: bold;">echo</span> <span style="color: #009c8f;">"0"</span> &gt; /dev/led/thinklight
</pre>
</div>

<p>
Then, I just added it to the <code>/usr/local/etc/doas.conf</code> to be able to
launch this script from root without entering the password. And
inserted corresponding command to the proper section of AwesomeWM
configuration:
</p>
<div class="org-src-container">
<pre class="src src-lua"><span style="color: #909995;">-- </span><span style="color: #909995;">Timer to show notification about low battery:</span>
gears.timer {
    timeout = 60,
    call_now = <span style="color: #0072d4; font-weight: bold;">true</span>,
    autostart = <span style="color: #0072d4; font-weight: bold;">true</span>,
    <span style="color: #0072d4;">callback</span> = <span style="color: #489100; font-weight: bold;">function</span>()
        vicious.call_async(
            vicious.widgets.bat, <span style="color: #009c8f;">"$2"</span>, <span style="color: #009c8f;">"0"</span>,
            <span style="color: #489100; font-weight: bold;">function</span>(<span style="color: #0072d4;">data</span>)
                <span style="color: #489100; font-weight: bold;">local</span> <span style="color: #0072d4;">battery_level</span> = <span style="color: #53676d; font-weight: bold;">tonumber</span>(data)
                <span style="color: #489100; font-weight: bold;">if</span> battery_level &lt; 4 <span style="color: #489100; font-weight: bold;">then</span>
                    naughty.notify {
                        text = <span style="color: #53676d; font-weight: bold;">string</span>.<span style="color: #53676d; font-weight: bold;">format</span>(<span style="color: #009c8f;">"Battery is low!\n%d%% is left!"</span>, battery_level),
                        urgency = <span style="color: #009c8f;">"critical"</span>,
                        position = <span style="color: #009c8f;">"top_middle"</span>,
                        icon = beautiful.icon_battery_0,
                        preset = naughty.config.presets.critical
                    }
                    awful.spawn.easy_async_with_shell(<span style="color: #009c8f;">"doas /home/drag0n/.bin/battery_sos.sh"</span>, <span style="color: #489100; font-weight: bold;">function</span>(<span style="color: #0072d4;">_</span>)
                    <span style="color: #489100; font-weight: bold;">end</span>)
                <span style="color: #489100; font-weight: bold;">end</span>
        <span style="color: #489100; font-weight: bold;">end</span>)
    <span style="color: #489100; font-weight: bold;">end</span>
}
</pre>
</div>

<p>
Done! Now, when the battery level is too low — I receive both
notification and blinking ThinkLight LED.
</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>